Projects keep NAHPU data organized. Each project holds its own sites,
collecting events, specimen records, narratives, associated data, and linked
media, while sharing personnel and taxonomy records from the NAHPU database.

## Creating a project

{% aside type="caution" %}

Projects using `Personnel` Field IDs need at least one person with the
`Cataloger` role and a registered personal field number. When you choose
`Personnel` in the wizard, a `Cataloger` step lets you pick or create one; if
you choose `Skip`, the wizard leaves your current `Field ID` setting unchanged.
See [Personnel](/en/usages/personnel/#adding-personnel-to-a-project).

{% /aside %}

From the home screen, select `Create project` to create a new project identity
or reuse project information from another device.

{% steps %}

1. On `Welcome`, select `Create new project` {% ref id="E" /%}. To reuse an
   existing project identity instead, choose `Import project-info JSON` {% ref id="F" /%}
   or, on a device with camera scanning (Android, iOS, macOS,
   or web), `Scan project QR` {% ref id="G" /%}.
2. Under `Project information`, enter a unique `Project name` (3–25
   characters). Optionally add a `Description` (up to 160 characters),
   `Principal investigator`, `Accession`, `Location`, IANA `Time zone`,
   `Start date`, and `End date`.
3. Under `Main taxon`, choose the catalog format for new specimen forms on this
   device. A single project can still hold specimens from multiple taxon
   groups. You can `Skip` this step and change it later in `Settings` >
   `Catalogs` > `Format`.
4. Under `Field ID`, choose `Personnel` or `Project` (or `Skip` and configure
   it later in `Settings` > `Catalogs` > `Specimens`):
   - **`Personnel`** uses an eligible cataloger's initials and running field
     number. Select an existing cataloger or create a new one on the next
     `Cataloger` step.
   - **`Project`** joins `Prefix`, `Current catalog number`, and `Suffix`
     directly with no inserted separators. Turn on `Auto-increment catalog
     number` to advance the number automatically.
5. On `Review`, check the project details, device setup, and taxonomy
   readiness, then select `Create project` to open the dashboard.

{% /steps %}

NAHPU assigns a UUID to every new project. On wide screens, wizard steps appear
in a left rail; on narrower screens, they appear as horizontal chips with the
same validation.

Importing project-info JSON or scanning a project QR code preserves the source
UUID and jumps straight to `Review`. It copies project metadata only—not
records, personnel, taxa, media, or device settings. Use a full project
transfer when records and media must also move between devices.

## Project time zone

`Time zone` records where the fieldwork takes place using an IANA name such as
`Asia/Jakarta` or `America/New_York`. Choose the fieldwork location's time
zone, not the time zone of the data-entry device.

**NAHPU never converts times.** Dates and times are stored exactly as entered
in the field's local clock. Opening the project in another time zone does not
shift stored times—it may only change how dates or 12/24-hour clocks display.
For example, with `Asia/Jakarta`, an event entered as **12 May, 08:30** always
shows `08:30` on every device, and the project time zone documents which clock
that `08:30` refers to.

- The time zone appears in `Project overview`, travels with project-info and
  project transfers, and is exposed as `project::timeZone` in templates and
  exports so downstream databases can convert local times to UTC.
- Changing `Time zone` never alters the device clock or rewrites existing
  records. Keep the same value on every device sharing a project.
- If a team spans multiple time zones or enters historical records from
  elsewhere, note exceptions in the project description or a narrative—or use a
  separate project per time zone.

## Managing projects from the home screen

When projects exist, the home screen provides a search bar, quick actions
(`Create project`, `Gallery`, `Import project`, and `Statistics`), and a
list/grid view toggle. Each project's three-dot menu includes:

| Action | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `Edit info` | Opens the project form to update metadata. |
| `Show QR` | Displays a QR code of the project metadata for another device to scan. |
| `Export info` | Saves project metadata as JSON (without records or media). |
| `Details` | Views project information without editing it. |

## Project dashboard

The `Project dashboard` has four panels and a bottom `Tools` dock:

- **`Project overview`** {% ref id="A" /%}: shows the project QR code and
  metadata grouped under `Identity`, `Description`, `Project details`,
  `Schedule`, and `Record metadata`. Use `Edit` {% ref id="B" /%} to update
  details or `Export info` {% ref id="C" /%} to export metadata as JSON.
- **`Personnel`** {% ref id="D" /%}: lists people assigned to the project, with
  `Add personnel`, `Manage`, edit icons, and selection controls.
- **`Taxon registry`**: shows counts of registered orders, families, species
  (and total taxa when higher-rank or subspecies entries exist), with `Manage`
  and `Add taxon`.
- **`Record statistics`**: summarizes project records and opens detailed
  statistics.
- **`Tools` dock**: provides quick launchers along the bottom of the dashboard
  (configured in `Settings` > `Tools` or via `Edit` at the end of the dock).

The project menu (a side rail on desktop, or a drawer on smaller screens)
provides `Merge project`, `Export project`, `Batch import`, `Bundle records`,
`Export records`, `Export documents`, `Backup database`, `Settings`,
`Export user configs`, `Import user configs`, and `Delete project`.

## Updating project information

Select `Edit` {% ref id="B" /%} in `Project overview` on the dashboard, or
choose `Edit info` from a project's home-screen menu. Fields are grouped under
`Project details`, `Place and time`, and `Project dates`. Change the active
catalog format or `Field ID` settings in [Settings](../settings).

## Sharing project information

Copy a project's identity and metadata to another device without transferring
records:

{% steps %}

1. On the source device, choose `Export info` or `Show QR` from the project's
   home-screen menu (or tap the QR code / `Export info` in `Project overview`).
2. On the receiving device, select `Create project`, then choose
   `Import project-info JSON` {% ref id="F" /%} or `Scan project QR` {% ref id="G" /%}
   on a device with camera scanning.

{% /steps %}

This preserves the source UUID and creates a matching project shell. To move
records, media, and linked data, use
[Project transfer](../export/project-transfer) instead.

## Transferring project data

To move records and media, run `Export project` from the project menu, then on
the receiving device:

| On the receiving device | Use | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Project does not exist yet | `Import project` on the home screen | Creates a new local project |
| Project already exists | `Merge project` in that project's menu | Merges imported records into the existing project |

ZIP and TAR.GZ exports include media; `Light export` (JSON.GZ) includes records
only. A NAHPU data package can also be imported or merged. Darwin Core packages
and tabular exports cannot restore a project. Keep the source archive until you
have verified the records and media on the receiving device. See
[Project transfer](../export/project-transfer) for full steps.

## Deleting a project

Open the project menu and select `Delete project`. NAHPU asks you to type the
first five characters of the project's UUID because deletion cannot be undone.

Deleting a project removes its specimen records and measurements, collecting
events (with collection methods, event personnel, and environmental data),
narratives, sites and coordinates, associated data, project-personnel links,
project media, stored project files, and the project record itself. Media still
referenced by a taxonomy record is detached rather than deleted. If file
cleanup fails, NAHPU shows a warning after removing the database records.

Export a project transfer or database backup first if you may need the data
later.

## Terminology

### Principal Investigator

The person responsible for the project.
